WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.497 --> 00:00:05.489
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:14.471 --> 00:00:18.463
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:08:48.391 --> 00:08:51.884
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:13.341 --> 00:09:16.834
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:17:08.928 --> 00:17:12.920
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:39.247 --> 00:18:42.740
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:22:52.739 --> 00:22:56.731
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:39.800 --> 00:23:43.430
Your program indicates
an intermission,

00:23:43.430 --> 00:23:44.390
but that is not true.

00:23:47.970 --> 00:23:50.300
I just have to say, the
two saxophone pieces

00:23:50.300 --> 00:23:54.710
that you heard so beautifully
played, that's all them.

00:23:54.710 --> 00:23:55.730
They picked the music.

00:23:55.730 --> 00:23:57.500
They did all the rehearsing.

00:23:57.500 --> 00:24:00.440
And I think they did a
fabulous job doing all that.

00:24:00.440 --> 00:24:04.320
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:07.720 --> 00:24:08.415
I welcome you.

00:24:08.415 --> 00:24:10.540
My name is Fred Harrison,
director of wind and jazz

00:24:10.540 --> 00:24:11.860
ensembles here at MIT.

00:24:11.860 --> 00:24:16.000
And although somewhat subtle and
somewhat not, what you heard,

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:18.220
those three pieces, is
actually a little bit

00:24:18.220 --> 00:24:21.740
of a tribute to John McLellan.

00:24:21.740 --> 00:24:22.240
Wow.

00:24:22.240 --> 00:24:24.760
Already?

00:24:24.760 --> 00:24:29.650
So I want to-- before I get
to that-- thank a few people.

00:24:29.650 --> 00:24:31.723
Some things will be
happening behind me.

00:24:31.723 --> 00:24:34.140
And so I'm going to talk while
those things are happening.

00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:36.098
I hope those things are
starting to happen now.

00:24:36.098 --> 00:24:38.440
Are they?

00:24:38.440 --> 00:24:41.980
Would be the cue,
start coming out now.

00:24:41.980 --> 00:24:44.420
While the full ensemble
makes its way to stage--

00:24:44.420 --> 00:24:47.110
cue-- I want to thank
some key people who

00:24:47.110 --> 00:24:48.950
have been at the heart
of our support system

00:24:48.950 --> 00:24:50.200
this entire academic year.

00:24:50.200 --> 00:24:53.800
Cuco Daglio, MIT Music and
Theater Arts multimedia

00:24:53.800 --> 00:24:55.150
specialist.

00:24:55.150 --> 00:24:56.170
You clap at the end.

00:24:56.170 --> 00:24:58.510
Tony DeBartolo, our
recording engineer.

00:24:58.510 --> 00:25:00.340
Kyleena Shallenberger
and her husband

00:25:00.340 --> 00:25:03.160
Isaiah, administrators for MTA.

00:25:03.160 --> 00:25:06.670
Michael Carmichael, oh, boy,
it's a long day and night.

00:25:06.670 --> 00:25:10.360
Michelle Carmichael,
administrative officer for MTA.

00:25:10.360 --> 00:25:15.490
Larry Gallagher and his entire
MIT video productions team that

00:25:15.490 --> 00:25:17.650
has made the dream of
streaming performances

00:25:17.650 --> 00:25:21.610
possible with the support
of Neil and Jane Pappalardo.

00:25:21.610 --> 00:25:24.875
On May 11th, we have a
unique musical event.

00:25:24.875 --> 00:25:26.750
You might see it on the
back of your program.

00:25:26.750 --> 00:25:29.350
You might have seen a rather
large poster up front.

00:25:29.350 --> 00:25:30.460
The Great Clarinet Summit.

00:25:30.460 --> 00:25:31.960
How many of you
already have tickets

00:25:31.960 --> 00:25:34.170
to the Great Clarinet Summit?

00:25:34.170 --> 00:25:38.880
I'll say half of you for our
visiting audience online.

00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:41.760
What is this concert?

00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:44.850
Yes, things are continuing
to happen behind me.

00:25:44.850 --> 00:25:48.180
So this is-- I'm not
aware of any other concert

00:25:48.180 --> 00:25:49.860
quite like this
that has happened.

00:25:49.860 --> 00:25:51.660
I won't go as far as
to say there's never

00:25:51.660 --> 00:25:54.700
been a concert like this,
but it could be the case.

00:25:54.700 --> 00:26:00.510
We have five major clarinet
soloists, three of whom

00:26:00.510 --> 00:26:05.250
have been longstanding
jazz icons

00:26:05.250 --> 00:26:08.070
of the clarinet for many years.

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:11.340
Billy Novick is a
Boston jazz institution.

00:26:11.340 --> 00:26:13.110
Clarinetist from
Lexington, Mass has

00:26:13.110 --> 00:26:15.840
been doing all of the
major clarinet jazz

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:18.390
work in the Boston
area for 30 years.

00:26:18.390 --> 00:26:21.390
Don Byron has been on the
scene, a graduate of New England

00:26:21.390 --> 00:26:25.140
Conservatory, has been on the
scene for at least 30 years

00:26:25.140 --> 00:26:28.380
and really one of the most
innovative and eclectic

00:26:28.380 --> 00:26:33.000
and soulful musicians
on the music scene.

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:38.070
Anat Cohen, Israeli-born
fantastic clarinetist

00:26:38.070 --> 00:26:41.640
is newer to the scene
and has been tearing up

00:26:41.640 --> 00:26:45.870
that scene in the world of
jazz and improvisational music.

00:26:45.870 --> 00:26:47.760
So that's three people.

00:26:47.760 --> 00:26:50.670
That would seem to be enough,
right, for a clarinet summit?

00:26:50.670 --> 00:26:55.080
But yet, we have two incredible
clarinetist on our faculty.

00:26:55.080 --> 00:26:58.290
Eran Egozy is newly
on our faculty,

00:26:58.290 --> 00:27:00.090
the last couple of years.

00:27:00.090 --> 00:27:04.920
Is a fantastic classical
clarinetist and founder

00:27:04.920 --> 00:27:07.800
of the Radius Chamber
Ensemble around town.

00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:10.560
And in his spare
time, he creates

00:27:10.560 --> 00:27:15.000
things like Guitar Hero
and also just newly

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:19.320
a new Boston Symphony Orchestra
program, online program note

00:27:19.320 --> 00:27:20.440
system.

00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:22.440
And of course, there
is Evan Ziporyn,

00:27:22.440 --> 00:27:24.870
who is one of the
leading clarinet

00:27:24.870 --> 00:27:29.490
players in the world,
particularly the bass clarinet.

00:27:29.490 --> 00:27:33.090
But really has
his own ensembles.

00:27:33.090 --> 00:27:35.800
Performs with Yo-Yo
Ma, among many others.

00:27:35.800 --> 00:27:40.950
And as I say, happens to be our
faculty head and chair, who's

00:27:40.950 --> 00:27:42.720
been on this faculty
for 25 years.

00:27:42.720 --> 00:27:45.690
And then on the poster
it says and you.

00:27:45.690 --> 00:27:46.680
And you.

00:27:46.680 --> 00:27:48.960
So how many of you play
the clarinet out there?

00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:50.220
Raise your hand.

00:27:50.220 --> 00:27:52.907
OK, how many of you have already
signed up for the summit?

00:27:52.907 --> 00:27:54.990
How many of you don't know
what I'm talking about?

00:27:54.990 --> 00:27:56.800
How many of you are fearful?

00:27:56.800 --> 00:27:59.130
So if you play the
clarinet, you're thinking,

00:27:59.130 --> 00:28:00.870
I'm going to play
with all these people?

00:28:00.870 --> 00:28:03.120
You just went on and on
about how wonderful they are.

00:28:03.120 --> 00:28:04.650
How am I going to
play with them?

00:28:04.650 --> 00:28:06.840
I've been playing
for five years.

00:28:06.840 --> 00:28:08.580
I've been playing
for three years.

00:28:08.580 --> 00:28:10.288
I've been playing for 45 years.

00:28:10.288 --> 00:28:11.580
And I'm in the Boston Symphony.

00:28:11.580 --> 00:28:12.960
Well, guess what?

00:28:12.960 --> 00:28:17.340
You can all play on this concert
because a fabulous musician,

00:28:17.340 --> 00:28:19.110
who I will introduce
a little bit later,

00:28:19.110 --> 00:28:21.150
Jamshied Sharifi
composed a piece

00:28:21.150 --> 00:28:25.860
called "Ornament Of The World"
that, in fact, will feature

00:28:25.860 --> 00:28:29.618
all five of these clarinetists,
plus Ini Oguntola,

00:28:29.618 --> 00:28:32.160
who is part of the inspiration
for the whole clarinet summit.

00:28:32.160 --> 00:28:35.790
He's a junior here at MIT and
a fabulous improvisational

00:28:35.790 --> 00:28:38.010
clarinetist.

00:28:38.010 --> 00:28:42.660
And the MIT Wind Ensemble and
the MIT Festival Jazz Ensemble.

00:28:42.660 --> 00:28:44.490
Somehow we will
often on this stage.

00:28:44.490 --> 00:28:47.370
And then I think we're up
to 70 people signed up now.

00:28:47.370 --> 00:28:49.650
I'm dreaming of 200 clarinets.

00:28:49.650 --> 00:28:52.110
I know it sounds scary, but
it's going to be beautiful.

00:28:52.110 --> 00:28:55.260
200 clarinets will be aligned.

00:28:55.260 --> 00:28:57.540
And they will play
in this piece.

00:28:57.540 --> 00:28:59.320
How does that happen?

00:28:59.320 --> 00:29:01.530
Well, my goodness
we're all ready.

00:29:01.530 --> 00:29:03.550
This is very exciting.

00:29:03.550 --> 00:29:07.530
OK, I can stop the commercial
for the clarinet summit now.

00:29:07.530 --> 00:29:10.920
And I know you
wanted more, but I

00:29:10.920 --> 00:29:13.590
believe there's a very
full MIT Wind Ensemble.

00:29:13.590 --> 00:29:18.450
And you might see faces that
are slightly older than college

00:29:18.450 --> 00:29:19.320
students.

00:29:19.320 --> 00:29:22.680
And that's because we have
sixth, seventh, and eighth

00:29:22.680 --> 00:29:25.360
graders from the Chenery
Middle School here.

00:29:25.360 --> 00:29:28.755
[APPLAUSE]

00:29:33.120 --> 00:29:36.690
They are here to honor
John McLellan, who

00:29:36.690 --> 00:29:41.100
is fantastic music educator
from the Belmont Public Schools.

00:29:41.100 --> 00:29:43.770
And he's retiring this year
after 35 years in Belmont

00:29:43.770 --> 00:29:47.730
and a number of years
in other places.

00:29:47.730 --> 00:29:50.430
Before I don't tell you
what we're going to play,

00:29:50.430 --> 00:29:52.650
I want to say something
about John McLellan.

00:29:52.650 --> 00:29:55.140
The MIT Wind Ensemble has
collaborated with the Chenery

00:29:55.140 --> 00:29:56.640
Middle School on
numerous occasions

00:29:56.640 --> 00:29:58.620
over the last 10, 15 years.

00:29:58.620 --> 00:30:00.930
And I've personally been
collaborating with John

00:30:00.930 --> 00:30:04.830
since 1987, first interning
at the Chenery School,

00:30:04.830 --> 00:30:06.480
and then later
teaching alongside him

00:30:06.480 --> 00:30:08.030
in the Belmont Public Schools.

00:30:08.030 --> 00:30:09.905
He's one of my dearest
friends and colleagues

00:30:09.905 --> 00:30:12.360
and among the greatest
musicians and music educators

00:30:12.360 --> 00:30:15.090
I've been privileged to know
and learn from and continue

00:30:15.090 --> 00:30:16.260
to learn from.

00:30:16.260 --> 00:30:18.030
His teaching legacy
in Belmont is

00:30:18.030 --> 00:30:20.430
rooted in excellence,
creativity, kindness,

00:30:20.430 --> 00:30:24.540
deep dedication, and true caring
for each individual student.

00:30:24.540 --> 00:30:27.540
And there have been
thousands of them.

00:30:27.540 --> 00:30:30.510
I'd like to invite two of
them to come to the stage

00:30:30.510 --> 00:30:33.600
to say a little bit about
one facet of John's teaching

00:30:33.600 --> 00:30:35.010
that they'd like to talk about.

00:30:35.010 --> 00:30:37.920
Please welcome Tilly
Jones and Irene Son.

00:30:37.920 --> 00:30:41.301
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:30:50.970 --> 00:30:53.250
Hello, my name is Irene.

00:30:53.250 --> 00:30:54.270
This is Tilly.

00:30:54.270 --> 00:30:57.630
And we are here with the
Chenery Wind Ensemble

00:30:57.630 --> 00:31:00.870
to celebrate our
teacher, John McLellan,

00:31:00.870 --> 00:31:04.230
and his accomplishments over
the past 35 years of teaching

00:31:04.230 --> 00:31:07.290
at the Chenery Middle School.

00:31:07.290 --> 00:31:08.970
I'm an eighth grader at Chenery.

00:31:08.970 --> 00:31:10.830
And Mr. McLellan's
been my teacher

00:31:10.830 --> 00:31:14.110
for the past five years now.

00:31:14.110 --> 00:31:16.810
The levels, which are the
reason we are here today,

00:31:16.810 --> 00:31:22.390
are a series of exercises that
Mr. McLellan created in 1999

00:31:22.390 --> 00:31:26.980
in order to challenge his
students to take music

00:31:26.980 --> 00:31:29.710
to another level.

00:31:29.710 --> 00:31:32.050
In order to pass
the levels, you have

00:31:32.050 --> 00:31:34.210
to record yourself
playing the exercise.

00:31:34.210 --> 00:31:37.060
Then submit them to Mr.
McLellan or Miss Phipps.

00:31:37.060 --> 00:31:39.400
Then they would decide whether
the level is good enough

00:31:39.400 --> 00:31:41.320
to pass, or they would
send it back to you

00:31:41.320 --> 00:31:43.550
so that you could try it again.

00:31:43.550 --> 00:31:45.650
Once you've passed
the basic 10 levels,

00:31:45.650 --> 00:31:49.580
then Mr. McLellan writes an
individualized level for you

00:31:49.580 --> 00:31:51.470
that includes
skills that he feels

00:31:51.470 --> 00:31:54.420
will push you forward
as a musician.

00:31:54.420 --> 00:31:58.410
In total, Mr. McLellan's
written over 550 levels that

00:31:58.410 --> 00:32:00.190
are all different in some way.

00:32:00.190 --> 00:32:04.600
And Tilly and I have each
passed about 110 levels.

00:32:04.600 --> 00:32:07.870
And people ask us why do
you pass so many levels?

00:32:07.870 --> 00:32:10.570
And to be really honest, we had
to sit down and think about it

00:32:10.570 --> 00:32:13.060
for a second.

00:32:13.060 --> 00:32:15.092
After thinking about
it for a long time,

00:32:15.092 --> 00:32:17.050
we came to the conclusion
that there are really

00:32:17.050 --> 00:32:19.010
two main reasons.

00:32:19.010 --> 00:32:20.920
The first reason is
that Mr. McLellan

00:32:20.920 --> 00:32:28.330
has helped us to learn
the feeling of achievement

00:32:28.330 --> 00:32:30.820
and Mr. McLellan's
endless efforts

00:32:30.820 --> 00:32:32.500
to keep up with all of us.

00:32:32.500 --> 00:32:38.242
And it has encouraged
us to keep playing music

00:32:38.242 --> 00:32:39.960
and reach for our goals.

00:32:43.120 --> 00:32:46.590
The levels are a unique way
to practice our instruments.

00:32:46.590 --> 00:32:49.980
And because all the levels
are different and challenging

00:32:49.980 --> 00:32:53.520
in their own way, it makes it
more interesting to practice

00:32:53.520 --> 00:32:55.560
the levels.

00:32:55.560 --> 00:33:01.530
I, as a player, have
improved very much.

00:33:01.530 --> 00:33:04.950
And I'm sure others here
feel the same way as I do.

00:33:04.950 --> 00:33:07.500
Mr. McLellan's writing has
really inspired us all.

00:33:07.500 --> 00:33:09.480
And the time and
thought that he devotes

00:33:09.480 --> 00:33:12.450
into writing each and every
level for all of his students

00:33:12.450 --> 00:33:15.197
is really incredible.

00:33:15.197 --> 00:33:18.620
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:22.540 --> 00:33:24.670
The second reason
we do levels is

00:33:24.670 --> 00:33:27.010
because levels are a way
that Mr. McLellan connects

00:33:27.010 --> 00:33:27.940
with his students.

00:33:27.940 --> 00:33:29.437
Mr. McLellan knows everyone.

00:33:29.437 --> 00:33:32.020
He doesn't just know their name
and what instrument they play.

00:33:32.020 --> 00:33:33.820
He understands
everyone's personality.

00:33:33.820 --> 00:33:36.340
And he knows how dedicated
to music they are.

00:33:36.340 --> 00:33:37.990
He knows so much
about everyone he's

00:33:37.990 --> 00:33:40.510
able to write a piece of
music that is specifically

00:33:40.510 --> 00:33:44.570
made to match their strengths,
weaknesses, and character.

00:33:44.570 --> 00:33:46.570
So many people I know
have just done the levels

00:33:46.570 --> 00:33:49.112
so that they could have a piece
of music specifically written

00:33:49.112 --> 00:33:51.650
for them written by
their favorite teacher.

00:33:51.650 --> 00:33:54.680
Mr. McLellan has
written over 550 levels,

00:33:54.680 --> 00:33:58.550
and so many people ask us
why we've done 110 levels.

00:33:58.550 --> 00:34:00.170
And although we've
done 110, we've

00:34:00.170 --> 00:34:02.000
only passed a fifth of
the amount of levels

00:34:02.000 --> 00:34:03.110
he's actually written.

00:34:03.110 --> 00:34:06.170
Mr. McLellan is one of the
most hardworking and inspiring

00:34:06.170 --> 00:34:07.250
teachers we've ever had.

00:34:07.250 --> 00:34:08.625
And we all just
want to thank him

00:34:08.625 --> 00:34:12.371
for making the Chenery a better
place for the past 35 years.

00:34:12.371 --> 00:34:15.808
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:24.449 --> 00:34:26.699
So I'm not going to tell you
what we're about to play.

00:34:26.699 --> 00:34:29.880
I will tell you that the
composer is John McLellan.

00:34:29.880 --> 00:34:32.310
The arranger is Kenneth Amos.

00:34:32.310 --> 00:34:35.070
The commissioning body of
this three movement work

00:34:35.070 --> 00:34:40.169
is POMS, Parents of Music
Students, Belmont's community

00:34:40.169 --> 00:34:41.850
and music support group.

00:34:41.850 --> 00:34:44.219
And we thank them for
making what you about

00:34:44.219 --> 00:34:45.100
to hear possible.

00:34:45.100 --> 00:34:47.580
And I want to thank POMS'
president, Kimberly Carlisle.

00:34:47.580 --> 00:34:50.550
And particularly, Cindy
Estes, membership chairperson

00:34:50.550 --> 00:34:52.199
of POMS who has been
at the front lines

00:34:52.199 --> 00:34:53.590
for this whole project.

00:34:53.590 --> 00:34:56.310
Lastly, a huge thanks
to Sharon Phipps,

00:34:56.310 --> 00:34:58.615
John's co-director of
the Chenery School, who

00:34:58.615 --> 00:35:03.570
put enormous energy, both
logistically and musically

00:35:03.570 --> 00:35:05.200
into this whole project.

00:35:05.200 --> 00:35:07.605
I also just want to thank
the Chenery sixth, seventh,

00:35:07.605 --> 00:35:09.480
and eighth graders who
participated in this--

00:35:09.480 --> 00:35:10.800
50 of them.

00:35:10.800 --> 00:35:13.140
They're amazing young
people, as you will hear.

00:35:13.140 --> 00:35:14.250
And you've already heard.

00:35:14.250 --> 00:35:15.900
And it was just a
complete joy for me

00:35:15.900 --> 00:35:18.300
to come back home, as
it were, to Belmont.

00:35:18.300 --> 00:35:19.620
Amazing young people.

00:35:19.620 --> 00:35:21.930
Please enjoy this
three movement tribute

00:35:21.930 --> 00:35:24.360
to John McLellan
arranged by Kenneth Amos

00:35:24.360 --> 00:35:28.714
and performed by the Chenery
and MIT Wind Ensembles.

00:35:28.714 --> 00:35:32.193
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:36.169 --> 00:35:40.145
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:45:30.226 --> 00:45:34.218
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:55.090 --> 00:46:56.820
How many of you
are from Belmont?

00:46:56.820 --> 00:47:00.580
[CHEERS]

00:47:00.580 --> 00:47:02.830
And there's 50
people behind me that

00:47:02.830 --> 00:47:06.670
knew all about this and
yet, not a word was spoken.

00:47:09.520 --> 00:47:12.845
[CHEERS AND APPLAUSE]

00:47:15.710 --> 00:47:20.860
And as I recognize levels
seven, eight, and nine,

00:47:20.860 --> 00:47:24.560
thankfully I stopped at level
five writing them for band.

00:47:24.560 --> 00:47:26.570
So now, I don't have
to worry about those.

00:47:26.570 --> 00:47:28.900
And I believe the woodwind
quintet a couple of months

00:47:28.900 --> 00:47:29.995
ago played level six.

00:47:29.995 --> 00:47:30.950
There you go.

00:47:30.950 --> 00:47:34.040
So we're almost done, everyone.

00:47:34.040 --> 00:47:35.752
Where's Ken Amos?

00:47:35.752 --> 00:47:36.970
Ken Amos?

00:47:36.970 --> 00:47:38.828
Come on, Ken Amos.

00:47:38.828 --> 00:47:40.120
He's up in the booth, actually.

00:47:40.120 --> 00:47:40.920
He can't come down.

00:47:40.920 --> 00:47:44.610
I would have hidden
there too, Ken.

00:47:44.610 --> 00:47:45.650
Congrats.

00:47:45.650 --> 00:47:48.770
Well, thank you
immensely, Ken Amos.

00:47:48.770 --> 00:47:50.920
But where's Sharon Phipps?

00:47:50.920 --> 00:47:52.170
She's not getting out of this.

00:47:52.170 --> 00:47:55.390
[CHEERS AND APPLAUSE]

00:47:55.903 --> 00:47:57.820
Sharon, if you don't
want to speak in the mic,

00:47:57.820 --> 00:48:02.450
maybe get Tilly and
Irene to talk for you.

00:48:02.450 --> 00:48:04.310
I am deeply impressed.

00:48:04.310 --> 00:48:08.270
Thank you very much, students
of Belmont, students of MIT,

00:48:08.270 --> 00:48:10.900
and the amazing Dr. Harris.

00:48:10.900 --> 00:48:12.970
But most of all, Sharon
Phipps, everyone.

00:48:12.970 --> 00:48:15.370
[CHEERS AND APPLAUSE]

00:48:23.453 --> 00:48:24.370
Now it's intermission.

00:48:24.370 --> 00:48:27.720
[APPLAUSE]

